#!/usr/bin/env sh
# Releases the board claims and file locks of worktrees that this merge put on main.
#
# WHY THIS IS A HOOK AND NOT A STEP IN THE LANDING RECIPE
# -------------------------------------------------------
# The failure it prevents is a claim that outlives the work it claimed. A chat that merges
# and stops leaves its claims behind, and because they are leases they block other agents
# for hours on files whose work is already on main. Nothing else ever takes them back, and
# "remember to release before you stop" is a step that is skipped exactly when it matters —
# by the sessions that died, and by the ones that merged and moved on.
#
# The script runs only on the base branch. It checks ORIG_HEAD against the base branch reflog
# and considers only clean branches newly reachable from the exact transition snapshot. If
# either previous-tip signal is unavailable or disagrees, the transition is reported as
# unproven and nothing is released. Board heartbeat age is not used as proof that an editor
# stopped. Releases are made as the merged branch's own identity, without `--force`.
#
# IT MUST NOT BE ABLE TO STOP A MERGE. By the time a post-merge hook runs the merge is
# already recorded, so failing here would not prevent anything — it would only leave the
# operator with a landed merge and a confusing error. The script reports to stderr and
# exits 0, and this file deliberately does not use `set -e` for the same reason: a gate that
# can block a landing is a gate that will eventually stop one.
#
# `HUSKY=0` is handled by the dispatcher in scripts/install-git-hooks.js, which also skips
# this file when the worktree predates it.
node scripts/merge-release.mjs || echo "merge-release: could not run; claims stay held, run it by hand" >&2
